From ab626f4f2cd3f931fb4559c5a3927b150fb06430 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 21:37:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Lifecycle guard: follow a pipe into a compound
 command's shell

`printf T | { echo x; bash; }` runs T in the group's shell, but the sink
regex's pipe alternative stops at the first `;`/newline, so
output_reaches_shell() said no sink and the printed text was never walked.
Under unknown alias names (`$'..'` anywhere, `alias q=r`) the nested
printed-pipeline walk is skipped on the premise that the session walk reads
the text, so `printf 't ( bash DB )' | { echo x; bash -l; }; echo $'y'` was
missed the same way. Heredoc producers (`python3 - <<'PY' | { :; bash; }`)
and `| while read l; do bash; done` had the same gap.

Five whys: the refusal is missing -> the printed literal is not walked ->
output_reaches_shell() is false -> the pipe alternative is a flat regex that
ends at list operators -> group structure was only modelled by the nested
(login-shell) walk, which is skipped under unknown names.

Fix: output_reaches_shell() also asks the printed-pipeline unit walk
(cron.lifecycle_guard_nested_shells.pipe_feeds_consumer) whether any command
reading a pipe -- including commands inside a compound command the pipe
feeds -- matches the sink regex as if piped into directly. Untokenizable text
fails closed. The nested walk's stdin predicate is now a parameter of the
same unit walk (default unchanged).

Proof: tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_grouped_pipe_shell.py -- 13 cases
red on base 5d9e537979, all 26 green with the fix via scripts/run_tests.sh;
clean twins (`printf ':' | { echo x; bash; }`, harmless `$'y'` variants,
`| { echo x; cat; }`, `| (cat); echo bash`) stay allowed. All 73 test files
importing shell_heredoc/lifecycle_guard/terminal_tool_guards pass (11008
tests). Real Bash: `printf 'echo FED\n' | { echo x; bash --noprofile
--norc; }; echo $'y'` prints x FED y.

Task: t_326b169246d7306a
